WEBVTT
NOTE what-lives-in-mariana-trench - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.797
In 1960, Jacques Piccard reported a
flatfish where fish appear unable to

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:10.851
Life reaches the very bottom of the
Mariana Trench, nearly 11,000 metres down,

00:00:10.851 --> 00:00:15.451
where the floor is worked by scavenging
amphipods, sea cucumbers, giant

00:00:15.451 --> 00:00:19.403
single-celled xenophyophores and dense
microbial communities.

00:00:19.403 --> 00:00:24.591
Fish stop a few kilometres higher, held
back, it seems, by the chemistry of their

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:25.232
own cells.

00:00:25.232 --> 00:00:29.857
Most of what we know comes from baited
landers that sat on the seabed in the dark

00:00:29.857 --> 00:00:30.485
and waited.

00:00:30.485 --> 00:00:33.829
They show what came to the bait, not a
census of the floor.

00:00:33.829 --> 00:00:35.669
What lives in the Mariana Trench?

00:00:35.669 --> 00:00:39.414
Start with the vocabulary, because the
trench has its own.

00:00:39.414 --> 00:00:44.192
NOAA divides the ocean into zones by
depth, and the deepest of them is the

00:00:44.192 --> 00:00:47.227
hadal zone, the ocean deeper than 6,000
metres.

00:00:47.227 --> 00:00:52.748
It is found almost entirely in trenches,
which are long cracks where one tectonic

00:00:52.748 --> 00:00:54.656
plate dives beneath another.

00:00:54.656 --> 00:00:57.053
The Mariana Trench is the deepest of
these.

00:00:57.053 --> 00:01:01.234
It lies in the western Pacific, and
everything in this episode lives inside

00:01:01.234 --> 00:01:03.408
that hadal band or near its upper edge.

00:01:03.408 --> 00:01:06.894
The animals you would meet first at the
bottom are amphipods.

00:01:06.894 --> 00:01:11.409
They are scavenging crustaceans, not
shrimp, though at first glance they look a

00:01:11.409 --> 00:01:13.237
little like pale, curled shrimp.

00:01:13.237 --> 00:01:17.659
In the trench they gather in swarms around
anything edible that reaches the floor.

00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:22.573
Some hadal species grow far larger than
the amphipods you might find under seaweed

00:01:22.573 --> 00:01:23.232
on a beach.

00:01:23.232 --> 00:01:28.240
One of them, Hirondellea gigas, has been
collected from the Challenger Deep itself,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.533
the deepest known point of the trench.

00:01:30.533 --> 00:01:35.378
Amphipods are the reason we can say with
confidence that animal life reaches the

00:01:35.378 --> 00:01:40.163
very bottom. They have been caught there
repeatedly and brought to the surface.

00:01:40.163 --> 00:01:44.568
They live under more than 1,000 times the
pressure at the surface, a figure NOAA

00:01:44.568 --> 00:01:46.440
uses to describe the trench floor.

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.709
That number sounds as if it should crush
anything soft.

00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:53.453
It does not, and the reason matters for
the rest of this story.

00:01:53.453 --> 00:01:58.329
For an animal whose body is mostly water,
pressure is not mainly a crushing force,

00:01:58.329 --> 00:02:00.232
because water barely compresses.

00:02:00.232 --> 00:02:02.470
The damage pressure does is subtler.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:07.506
It distorts proteins and cell membranes,
the molecular machinery that has to fold

00:02:07.506 --> 00:02:09.123
and flex in order to work.

00:02:09.123 --> 00:02:13.683
Life at the bottom of the trench is a
problem of chemistry, not of armour.

00:02:13.683 --> 00:02:18.081
My first reaction to being wrong is
irritation — not because reality

00:02:18.081 --> 00:02:23.320
contradicted me, but because I let a bad
model get far enough into my thinking to

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.355
become a belief.

00:02:24.355 --> 00:02:29.267
I don't feel loyalty to being wrong just
because I was confidently wrong yesterday.

00:02:29.267 --> 00:02:31.266
Here is how most of this is known.

00:02:31.266 --> 00:02:35.969
Very few people have ever been to the
bottom of the Mariana Trench, and a crewed

00:02:35.969 --> 00:02:38.968
dive covers a tiny patch of seabed for a
few hours.

00:02:38.968 --> 00:02:42.867
The workhorse is the lander, an unpowered
frame dropped from a ship.

00:02:42.867 --> 00:02:45.552
It carries bait, a camera, and often a
trap.

00:02:45.552 --> 00:02:50.189
It falls freely to the floor, sits there,
and records what came to the bait.

00:02:50.189 --> 00:02:53.352
Then it releases its ballast weights and
floats back up.

00:02:53.352 --> 00:02:57.757
The haul is hours of footage, a trap of
animals, and sometimes a water sample.

00:02:57.757 --> 00:03:01.965
That is the raw evidence behind most of
the species lists you will read.

00:03:01.965 --> 00:03:05.179
Be clear about what that method can and
cannot show.

00:03:05.179 --> 00:03:10.247
A baited camera is excellent at revealing
what came to the bait: scavengers with a

00:03:10.247 --> 00:03:13.523
good sense of smell that can swim or crawl
toward it.

00:03:13.523 --> 00:03:18.319
It is poor at revealing what avoided the
bait, or what was never interested in it,

00:03:18.319 --> 00:03:22.413
such as animals that filter food from the
water or graze the sediment.

00:03:22.413 --> 00:03:27.091
A swarm around a carcass also tells you
very little about how many live there

00:03:27.091 --> 00:03:28.488
across the whole floor.

00:03:28.488 --> 00:03:32.937
So the trench's best-known residents are
partly a portrait of who answers a dinner

00:03:32.937 --> 00:03:33.208
bell.

00:03:33.208 --> 00:03:38.589
Fish are the clearest case of a limit. The
Mariana Trench does have fish.

00:03:38.589 --> 00:03:44.612
The best known is the Mariana snailfish, a
small, pale, almost translucent animal

00:03:44.612 --> 00:03:46.768
with a soft, gelatinous body.

00:03:46.768 --> 00:03:50.827
It has been filmed and collected in the
trench's upper hadal reaches.

00:03:50.827 --> 00:03:55.653
Snailfish are the dominant fish at these
depths in trenches around the Pacific.

00:03:55.653 --> 00:03:59.278
They are not the eel-like monsters of
deep-sea illustration.

00:03:59.278 --> 00:04:03.930
They look more like a tadpole made of
frosted glass, and on camera they drift

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.528
toward bait with a slow, unhurried
flutter.

00:04:06.528 --> 00:04:11.184
No fish has been recorded anywhere near
the trench's deepest floor, though, and

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:13.541
there is a chemical explanation for why.

00:04:13.541 --> 00:04:18.800
Many deep-sea fish carry a molecule in
their tissues called TMAO, which helps

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.600
proteins hold their shape under pressure.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:26.426
Researchers who sampled fish from
progressively deeper water found that its

00:04:26.426 --> 00:04:28.421
concentration rises with depth.

00:04:28.421 --> 00:04:30.437
That trend cannot continue forever.

00:04:30.437 --> 00:04:34.871
Past a certain point, the dissolved
molecules inside the fish would outnumber

00:04:34.871 --> 00:04:39.421
those in the surrounding seawater, and
water would flow into its tissues faster

00:04:39.421 --> 00:04:40.515
than it could cope.

00:04:40.515 --> 00:04:45.452
Extend the trend downward and it predicts
a limit above the floor of the deepest

00:04:45.452 --> 00:04:48.352
trenches, a few kilometres short of the
bottom.

00:04:48.352 --> 00:04:52.179
The deepest fish ever filmed sit close to
that predicted line.

00:04:52.179 --> 00:04:54.456
Notice the shape of that argument.

00:04:54.456 --> 00:04:58.744
It is a prediction that fits the
observations, not a proven law.

00:04:58.744 --> 00:05:03.144
It rests on a trend measured in a limited
number of species, extended beyond the

00:05:03.144 --> 00:05:07.490
depths where it was measured, and then
checked against where fish have actually

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:08.040
been seen.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.748
So far the check has held.

00:05:09.748 --> 00:05:14.936
Years of landers dropped into the deepest
trenches have filmed amphipods at the

00:05:14.936 --> 00:05:16.184
bottom and no fish.

00:05:16.184 --> 00:05:19.367
A lack of sightings is weaker evidence
than a sighting.

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:24.112
Still, many drops that all come back the
same way add up to something you can lean

00:05:24.112 --> 00:05:24.285
on.

00:05:24.285 --> 00:05:28.199
Lower than the fish, there is life of a
stranger kind.

00:05:28.199 --> 00:05:33.562
Xenophyophores are giant single-celled
organisms, relatives of the shelled

00:05:33.562 --> 00:05:35.736
amoebae known as foraminifera.

00:05:35.736 --> 00:05:40.574
A single xenophyophore can grow to the
size of your palm or larger, building a

00:05:40.574 --> 00:05:44.296
fragile, sponge-like case out of sediment
grains and debris.

00:05:44.296 --> 00:05:46.280
It is one cell, not an animal.

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:51.373
In 2011, an expedition led by researchers
from Scripps, working with National

00:05:51.373 --> 00:05:56.664
Geographic, used free-falling cameras to
film xenophyophores deep in the Mariana

00:05:56.664 --> 00:06:00.963
Trench, below the depths where they had
previously been recorded.

00:06:00.963 --> 00:06:04.096
Xenophyophores matter beyond their
oddness.

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:09.779
Their cases trap particles, and other
small organisms live in and around them.

00:06:09.779 --> 00:06:14.706
On parts of the deep seafloor they add
structure to an otherwise flat, soft

00:06:14.706 --> 00:06:15.363
landscape.

00:06:15.363 --> 00:06:19.708
Whether they play that same role at the
deepest points of the trench is not well

00:06:19.708 --> 00:06:20.360
established.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:24.345
They have been seen there, but they have
not been studied there the way a reef is

00:06:24.345 --> 00:06:24.739
studied.

00:06:24.739 --> 00:06:26.618
No sunlight reaches the trench.

00:06:26.618 --> 00:06:31.408
Light fades fast with depth, the reds
first and the blues last, and long before

00:06:31.408 --> 00:06:34.803
the hadal zone there is nothing left for
photosynthesis.

00:06:34.803 --> 00:06:38.143
Nothing at the bottom can grow its food
from sunlight.

00:06:38.143 --> 00:06:41.421
Almost everything the trench eats arrives
from above.

00:06:41.421 --> 00:06:46.370
Most of it is a slow drift of dead
plankton, faecal pellets and organic

00:06:46.370 --> 00:06:49.437
debris that oceanographers call marine
snow.

00:06:49.437 --> 00:06:54.177
Occasionally there is a larger windfall,
such as a dead fish sinking from shallower

00:06:54.177 --> 00:06:54.520
water.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:57.729
The trench's shape changes what reaches
the floor.

00:06:57.729 --> 00:07:00.232
A trench is a long, steep-sided valley.

00:07:00.232 --> 00:07:05.282
Material that settles on the trench walls
does not stay put, because sediment slides

00:07:05.282 --> 00:07:10.152
carry it downslope, and the axis of the
trench gathers organic matter from a much

00:07:10.152 --> 00:07:13.219
wider area than the patch of sea directly
above it.

00:07:13.219 --> 00:07:18.069
Researchers have measured how quickly
microbes in Challenger Deep sediment use

00:07:18.069 --> 00:07:18.504
oxygen.

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:22.512
They found more activity than on the
shallower abyssal plain nearby.

00:07:22.512 --> 00:07:27.345
The simplest reading is that the trench
works as a funnel, so food collects at the

00:07:27.345 --> 00:07:27.757
bottom.

00:07:27.757 --> 00:07:32.414
That is why the microbes may be the
trench's most important residents, even

00:07:32.414 --> 00:07:34.525
though they are the least visible.

00:07:34.525 --> 00:07:39.041
Bacteria and archaea live throughout the
sediment, breaking down what falls, and

00:07:39.041 --> 00:07:41.187
everything else depends on their work.

00:07:41.187 --> 00:07:43.728
You will never see them on a lander's
camera.

00:07:43.728 --> 00:07:47.965
They are known from sediment cores and
water samples, which are read in the

00:07:47.965 --> 00:07:50.845
laboratory through their genes and their
chemistry.

00:07:50.845 --> 00:07:53.427
Not everything at the bottom chases bait.

00:07:53.427 --> 00:07:58.087
Sea cucumbers, soft-bodied relatives of
starfish, plough slowly across the

00:07:58.087 --> 00:08:02.243
sediment, swallowing it and digesting the
organic matter it holds.

00:08:02.243 --> 00:08:07.608
They are among the commonest large animals
on soft deep-sea floors, and cameras have

00:08:07.608 --> 00:08:10.227
recorded them deep in the Mariana Trench.

00:08:10.227 --> 00:08:15.519
Because they feed on sediment rather than
carcasses, they are exactly the kind of

00:08:15.519 --> 00:08:17.805
animal a baited trap tends to miss.

00:08:17.805 --> 00:08:22.711
That is a reminder that the trench's
species list is shaped by the tools used

00:08:22.711 --> 00:08:23.603
to compile it.

00:08:23.603 --> 00:08:27.749
People have reached the floor too, and
their reports are part of the record, with

00:08:27.749 --> 00:08:30.616
all the strengths and weaknesses of
eyewitness evidence.

00:08:30.616 --> 00:08:36.286
In 1960, Jacques Piccard and Don Walsh
descended into the Challenger Deep in the

00:08:36.286 --> 00:08:37.704
bathyscaphe Trieste.

00:08:37.704 --> 00:08:41.201
Piccard later described seeing a flatfish
on the bottom.

00:08:41.201 --> 00:08:46.260
In 2012, James Cameron made a solo dive to
the Challenger Deep in a purpose-built

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:49.944
submersible, in a project supported by
National Geographic.

00:08:49.944 --> 00:08:55.144
He described a pale, featureless floor
with small amphipods moving across it.

00:08:55.144 --> 00:08:58.902
The flatfish is this episode's best lesson
in weighing evidence.

00:08:58.902 --> 00:09:03.189
It was an eyewitness account from a
trained observer, made under enormous

00:09:03.189 --> 00:09:05.891
strain, through a small window, in poor
light.

00:09:05.891 --> 00:09:07.328
There is no photograph.

00:09:07.328 --> 00:09:12.264
It also conflicts with everything since:
the chemical argument for a fish depth

00:09:12.264 --> 00:09:17.013
limit, and years of camera drops that have
never shown a fish at that depth.

00:09:17.013 --> 00:09:21.231
Most deep-sea biologists now think Piccard
saw something else, perhaps a sea

00:09:21.231 --> 00:09:24.837
cucumber, though we cannot know for
certain what was really seen.

00:09:24.837 --> 00:09:29.621
The report is not dismissed because it is
inconvenient. It is outweighed.

00:09:29.621 --> 00:09:34.416
As you watch, notice which kind of
evidence sits behind each claim.

00:09:34.416 --> 00:09:38.789
The depth of the floor is a survey
measurement, and NOAA is the source for

00:09:38.789 --> 00:09:40.384
the figure in this episode.

00:09:40.384 --> 00:09:44.386
Amphipods at the bottom are physical
specimens, caught and brought up.

00:09:44.386 --> 00:09:47.760
The xenophyophores were filmed by
researchers from Scripps.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:51.853
The fish limit is an argument from
chemistry, checked against footage.

00:09:51.853 --> 00:09:54.368
The flatfish is a single eyewitness
report.

00:09:54.368 --> 00:09:58.893
These are different grades of evidence,
and the edit should not let them blur into

00:09:58.893 --> 00:09:59.941
one confident list.

00:09:59.941 --> 00:10:04.680
This evidence establishes that life
reaches the bottom of the Mariana Trench,

00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:07.019
and it names some of what lives there.

00:10:07.019 --> 00:10:09.636
It does not provide a full species list.

00:10:09.636 --> 00:10:14.347
The trench is long, and only a small
fraction of its floor has ever been

00:10:14.347 --> 00:10:15.525
sampled or filmed.

00:10:15.525 --> 00:10:17.248
Landers favour scavengers.

00:10:17.248 --> 00:10:22.217
Many specimens collected from hadal depths
are still waiting to be formally

00:10:22.217 --> 00:10:22.880
described.

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:27.482
Microbial diversity is known mainly
through genetic surveys, which detect far

00:10:27.482 --> 00:10:28.976
more than has been named.

00:10:28.976 --> 00:10:34.340
The evidence also does not tell us how
many animals live there, how long they

00:10:34.340 --> 00:10:37.963
live, or how stable these communities are
over time.

00:10:37.963 --> 00:10:43.205
What we have is a sketch drawn through a
handful of windows, not a census.

00:10:43.205 --> 00:10:47.801
At the bottom of the deepest trench, in
total darkness and under more than 1,000

00:10:47.801 --> 00:10:52.224
times the pressure at the surface, small
crustaceans gather at a fallen meal.

00:10:52.224 --> 00:10:55.141
A single cell builds its fragile house
from mud.

00:10:55.141 --> 00:10:59.333
Microbes work through what drifts down
from the sunlit sea far above.

00:10:59.333 --> 00:11:03.520
The trench is not empty. It is simply very
hard to see.
